{#
  janitorr's verify Job and the ConfigMap that carries its script.

  The renderer emits `templates/verify/*.yaml.j2` into `janitorr-verify` (`verify_files`), which
  Flux applies once the `janitorr` Kustomization reads Ready. The manifests role takes only
  top-level `templates/*.yaml.j2`, so no Ansible path renders it. The renderer passes
  `flux_job_image` and `flux_job_connect`, the helper's text, to every `templates/verify/` file
  (`render_tag`).

  The Job runs `files/verify_janitorr.py`, which replaced the three proofs of `tasks/verify.yml`
  at janitorr's flip, with no `pods/exec` grant; the script's docstring names how each one
  moved. Both names end in a digest, as the Job census requires: the ConfigMap's in
  `configmap_digest8` of its data, the Job's in `job_spec_digest8` of its spec.

  The spec embeds a digest of the inputs that roll janitorr's pod, the ones `checksum/restart`
  hashes under Flux (`scripts/renderer/renderer_lib/restart.py`): the rendered Deployment, the
  `janitorr-config` Secret template and the config it renders, and the `cluster-vars.source`
  digest of each key those two read. So every change that rolls the pod renames the Job, and
  Flux runs it again, as Ansible runs `verify.yml` on every deploy.
  `tests/test_verify_janitorr.py` fails if a ConfigMap or Secret the pod names drops out.

  DECIDED: the pod carries `app: janitorr-verify`, never `app: janitorr`. The health reader
  (`files/janitorr_health.py`) reads the logs of the first Running pod labelled
  `app=janitorr`, so a Job pod under that label could be the pod it reads. The reach leg
  therefore proves the apps answer `janitorr-verify`, not `janitorr`; the render test holds
  sonarr's and radarr's fences admitting `janitorr`.

  DECIDED: the media mounts copy jellyfin's two `media` subPath mounts as literals, made
  read-write, and are not looked up from the jellyfin role. A lookup into another role would
  render jellyfin's Deployment without jellyfin's defaults. `tests/test_verify_janitorr.py`
  holds the literals equal to jellyfin's rendered mounts, so a jellyfin mount change fails
  that test until it is copied here, and the copy renames the Job.

  The caller census (D5, *A fence admits every Job that dials through it*). The pod is
  `app: janitorr-verify` in `k8s_namespace`. It execs into nothing and dials:
    - sonarr:8989, through sonarr's role-owned fence, whose `netpol_from` lists
      `janitorr-verify` ahead of the flip. #5709 added the peer in a PR of its own, because
      `scripts/validate/cross_plane_order.py` refuses one PR that changes janitorr and the
      flux-owned sonarr it depends on. netpol-baseline's sonarr row (`expect: refused`)
      shows that fence refuses an unlisted pod;
    - radarr:7878, the same through radarr's fence and its netpol-baseline row;
    - jellyfin:8096, which `networkpolicy-jellyfin.yaml.j2` opens to every source for jellyfin's
      LAN clients, so it has no peer to add and no refused leg to show.
  Without a peer, the helper's 60 seconds of refused connects run out and the Job fails
  closed. It also mounts the `janitorr-config` Secret's `application.yml`, which holds the
  *arr and Jellyfin API keys janitorr itself reads, and the `media-data` claim read-write. The
  claim's local PersistentVolume pins the pod to the claim's node.
#}
{% set script_name = 'verify_janitorr.py' %}
{% set script_dir = '/job' %}
{% set data = {
  script_name: lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/roles/k8s/janitorr/files/' + script_name, rstrip=False),
  'flux_job_connect.py': flux_job_connect,
} %}
{% set configmap_name = 'janitorr-verify-' + ({'data': data, 'binaryData': {}} | to_json(sort_keys=True, separators=(',', ':')) | hash('sha256'))[:8] %}
{#- The pod's roll inputs, as `checksum/restart` hashes them; see the note above. The Secret
    template and the config it renders are read raw, never rendered, and `cluster-vars.source`
    holds a digest of each key's ciphertext, so no value enters the hash. -#}
{% set roll = namespace(
  text=lookup('template', 'deployment.yaml.j2'),
  secrets=lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/roles/k8s/janitorr/templates/config-secret.yaml.j2')
    + lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/roles/k8s/janitorr/templates/config/application.yml.j2'),
) %}
{% for line in lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/vars/cluster-vars.source').splitlines() if line and not line.startswith('#') and ('{{ ' + line.split()[0] + ' ') in roll.secrets %}
{% set roll.text = roll.text + line %}
{% endfor %}
{% set roll_digest8 = ((roll.text + roll.secrets) | hash('sha256'))[:8] %}
{% set spec_text %}
# A flaky check fails rather than passing on a retry, and a retry would write a second probe.
backoffLimit: 0
# Below the 600s `manifests_rollout_timeout_default` every `<tag>-verify` Kustomization takes.
# Three reach legs of up to 60s of connect retries each, and the walk to the first episode.
activeDeadlineSeconds: 300
template:
  metadata:
    labels:
      # See the first DECIDED note above.
      app: janitorr-verify
  spec:
    serviceAccountName: janitorr-verify
    # The Job reads no API, so its pod holds no token.
    automountServiceAccountToken: false
    restartPolicy: Never
    enableServiceLinks: false
    # The uid janitorr and jellyfin both run the library as, so the read leg reads as they do.
    # Deliberately NO fsGroup, unlike janitorr's own pod: on this claim it would make the
    # kubelet chown the whole library on every mount, as media-volume's verify Job records.
    securityContext:
      runAsNonRoot: true
      runAsUser: {{ puid }}
      runAsGroup: {{ pgid }}
      seccompProfile:
        type: RuntimeDefault
    containers:
      - name: verify
        image: {{ flux_job_image }}
        command:
          - python3
          - {{ script_dir }}/{{ script_name }}
        env:
          - name: JANITORR_CONFIG
            value: /config/application.yml
          - name: JANITORR_EXPECT_DRY_RUN
            value: "{{ janitorr_k8s_dry_run | lower }}"
          # The base URLs janitorr's config dials, each with a path that answers without a key.
          - name: JANITORR_TARGETS
            value: http://sonarr:8989/ping http://radarr:7878/ping http://jellyfin:8096/health
          - name: MEDIA_ROOT
            value: /data
          # Renames the Job whenever janitorr's pod rolls; see the note above.
          - name: JANITORR_ROLL_DIGEST8
            value: "{{ roll_digest8 }}"
          - name: PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE
            value: "1"
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 10m
            memory: 32Mi
          limits:
            memory: 64Mi
        securityContext:
          allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
          readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
          capabilities:
            drop:
              - ALL
        volumeMounts:
          - name: script
            mountPath: {{ script_dir }}
            readOnly: true
          # janitorr's own mount of its config: the same Secret key at the same path.
          - name: janitorr-config
            mountPath: /config/application.yml
            subPath: application.yml
            readOnly: true
          # jellyfin's two media mounts, read-write so the probe link can be written; see the
          # second DECIDED note above.
          - name: media
            mountPath: /data
            subPath: media
          - name: media
            mountPath: /data/media
            subPath: media
    volumes:
      - name: script
        configMap:
          name: {{ configmap_name }}
      - name: janitorr-config
        secret:
          secretName: janitorr-config
      - name: media
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: {{ janitorr_k8s_media_claim }}
{% endset %}
{% set spec = spec_text | from_yaml %}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: {{ configmap_name }}
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
data:
{% for key, text in data | dictsort %}
  {{ key }}: {{ text | to_json }}
{% endfor %}
---
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: janitorr-verify-{{ (spec | to_json(sort_keys=True, separators=(',', ':')) | hash('sha256'))[:8] }}
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
  annotations:
    homelab/job-kind: verify
spec:
{{ spec_text | indent(2, true) }}
